"""`props`, written the way basedpython offers to write it

the pair is the point. `props` declares a `@property` and a `@v.setter` by hand
over a `_v` the class initialises itself; this declares `var v: int = 0` with a
`get`/`set` block under it, and the transpiler emits that same pair over a
backing `__v`. the work `run` does is `props`'s exactly, character for
character, so whatever the two rows differ by is the surface syntax and nothing
else

that is worth measuring rather than assuming, because a lowering is free only if
it lands on the shape the backend already knew. `props_ext` is the standing
warning here: it is `props` with one class added and it reads a fraction of it

no `setup` — the cell is one object and building it is not the measurement,
which is `fields`'s job
"""


class Cell:
    var v: int = 0
        get() = field
        set(given):
            field = given


def run(cell: Cell, n: int) -> int:
    i = 0
    while i < n:
        cell.v = cell.v + 1
        i = i + 1
    return cell.v


def bench() -> int:
    return run(Cell(), 300000)
